    {
      "id": "STY-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Stable real nonboundaries survive to the rank where invariant forms detect them",
      "anchor": "inputs-stable-real-cycles",
      "statement": "In degree m=k+1 and n≥2m+4, stabilization over the same Dedekind ring reflects real boundaries and every real cycle at a larger rank lifts up to a real boundary. In the odd-degree arithmetic application the reflected nonboundary at the final filter level survives at a rank where invariant forms represent the evaluations.",
      "hypotheses": "The stabilized chains and comparison with real homology are available. Fix a totally complex number field L, odd m=k+1<r₂(L) and a positive block multiplicity. A real cochain separates a nonboundary, an invariant form represents its evaluations after stabilization, and block weights determine which part survives.",
      "definitions": [
        "θ: Z_m(Γ;ℝ) → ℝ⊗ℤ H_m(Γ;ℤ) is surjective, natural, and has precisely the real boundaries as kernel.",
        "At level ℓ^k n the stabilizing map is injective on real homology. The two filter maps are block repetition and identity padding.",
        "In degree three over ℚ(ζ₁₆), r₂=4 and at ℓ=2 the two levels from initial size 10 are 20 and 40: test multipliers are 12,0,0.",
        "The fixed-size affine block comparison is used over the same field. SBridgeAlt also defines the proposition SBig. The selected arithmetic change of field is documented at FCT-02."
      ],
